/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - X(ω) = ∫−∞∞ x(t)e−jωtdt
   - Here x(t) is the signal; X(ω) is its spectrum; ω is angular frequency in rad/s; j is the imaginary unit.
   - The table gives the rules used most often.
   - For real x(t), the spectrum obeys X(−ω)=X*(ω).
   - Convolution in time is defined as y(t)=x(t)*h(t)=∫−∞∞x(τ)h(t−τ)dτ.
   - x(t)*h(t) ↔ X(ω)H(ω)
   - Here x(t) is the input; h(t) is the impulse response; X(ω) and H(ω) are their spectra; * means convolution.
   - The dual rule is multiplication in time: x(t)y(t) ↔ (1/2π)X(ω)*Y(ω).
   - Use Euler's identity cos(ω₀t)=(ejω₀t+e−jω₀t)/2.
   - x(t)cos(ω₀t) ↔ ½[X(ω−ω₀)+X(ω+ω₀)]
*/
