/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - For a continuous-time signal x(t), define the total energy E and the average power P by the limits below.
   - E = ∫−∞∞ |x(t)|² dt, P = limT→∞ (1/T) ∫−T/2T/2 |x(t)|² dt
   - Here x(t) is the signal; E is energy in joules (J) if x is in volts across 1 Ω; P is average power in watts (W); T is the averaging window length in seconds (s).
   - An energy signal has 0 < E < ∞.
   - These names decide which Parseval form applies.
   - Take the Fourier transform pair X(ω)=∫x(t)e−jωtdt and x(t)=(1/2π)∫X(ω)ejωtdω.
   - E = ∫−∞∞ |x(t)|² dt = (1/2π) ∫−∞∞ |X(ω)|² dω
   - Here E is total energy in J; X(ω) is the Fourier transform of x(t); |X(ω)|² is the energy spectral density; the factor 1/2π comes from the inverse transform convention above.
   - For a periodic signal with period T₀ and exponential Fourier series x(t)=Σ cₙ ej n ω₀ t, average power is the sum of squared coefficient magnitudes.
   - P = (1/T₀) ∫T₀ |x(t)|² dt = Σn=−∞∞ |cₙ|²
*/
