/* book Worked example: Q: For x(t)=e−3tu(t), compute the total energy E from the time waveform. Then verify Parseval's theorem using X(ω)=1/(3+jω), and find the band |ω| ≤ B that holds 90% of the energy.
   1. For t≥0, |x(t)|²=e−6t; for t<0 it is zero. Thus E=∫0∞ e−6tdt.
   2. ∫0∞ e−6tdt = [−e−6t/6]0∞ = 0 − (−1/6)=1/6 J.
   3. |X(ω)|² = 1/|3+jω|² = 1/(9+ω²).
   4. Parseval side: (1/2π)∫−∞∞ 1/(9+ω²)dω. Use ∫du/(a²+u²)=(1/a)tan−1(u/a). Here a=3, so the integral over all ω is π/3.
   5. (1/2π)(π/3)=1/6 J. Both methods give 0.1667 J.
   6. Energy inside |ω| ≤ B: (1/2π)∫−BBdω/(9+ω²) = (1/2π)(2/3)tan−1(B/3). As a fraction of E = 1/6 this is (2/π)tan−1(B/3).
   7. Set (2/π)tan−1(B/3) = 0.9: tan−1(B/3) = 0.45π rad = 81°, so B = 3 tan 81° = 3 × 6.314 = 18.94 rad/s (about 3.0 Hz).
   Answer: E = 1/6 J ≈ 0.1667 J from both sides, so the theorem is verified; 90% of the energy lies in |ω| ≤ 18.94 rad/s. */
