/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - For an LTI system with impulse response h(t), the frequency response is the Fourier transform of h(t).
   - H(ω)=Y(ω)/X(ω)=∫−∞∞ h(t)e−jωtdt
   - Here H(ω) is the complex frequency response; Y(ω) and X(ω) are Fourier transforms of output y(t) and input x(t); h(t) is the impulse response; j=√−1; ω is angular frequency in rad/s.
   - H(ω) is complex at each ω.
   - x(t)=A cos(ω₀t+φ) → y(t)=A|H(ω₀)| cos(ω₀t+φ+∠H(ω₀))
   - Here A is input amplitude in volts or another unit; ω₀ is input angular frequency in rad/s; φ is input phase in radians or degrees; y(t) is the steady-state output.
   - Some syllabi call this frequency-domain system analysis.
   - Use h(t) when it is known.
   - Use the differential equation when it is known.
   - y′(t)+a y(t)=b x(t) → H(ω)=b/(jω+a)
*/
