/* book Worked example 2: Q: The same RC low-pass (RC = 1 ms) receives x(t) = 2 + 3 cos(2000t) V. Find the steady-state output.
   1. Treat each frequency on its own (superposition). The input has a DC part (ω = 0) and a cosine at ω = 2000 rad/s.
   2. DC: H(0) = 1/(1+0) = 1, so the 2 V passes unchanged.
   3. At ω = 2000 rad/s: ωRC = 2000 × 0.001 = 2, so H = 1/(1+j2).
   4. |H| = 1/√(1+4) = 1/√5 = 0.4472; ∠H = −tan−12 = −63.43°.
   5. Cosine output amplitude: 3 × 0.4472 = 1.342 V, phase −63.4°.
   6. Add the parts: y(t) = 2 + 1.342 cos(2000t − 63.4°) V.
   Answer: y(t) = 2 + 1.34 cos(2000t − 63.4°) V: the DC passes untouched while the 2000 rad/s part is cut to 44.7% and delayed. */
